From 98d4eebf416565f08fba82713326bf1a0537a843 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: medusa build <build@medusa.local>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 06:18:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(wallet): take the imported signing key on stdin, not
 argv

`account import public --private-key <hex>` puts a raw 32-byte signing key on the
process command line, and /proc/<pid>/cmdline is world-readable (mode 0444, and a
stock Linux box mounts /proc with no hidepid), so any local uid can read the key for
as long as the import runs. Medusa's wallet wrapper re-execs its argv verbatim, so in
practice the key sat on TWO command lines at once.

Every other secret this CLI handles already avoids argv: read_password_from_stdin()
and read_mnemonic_from_stdin() in cli/mod.rs, and helperfunctions.rs states the intent
outright ("so the phrase never appears in argv, shell history, or `ps`"). `import
public` was simply never given the same treatment: private_key was a required clap
argument with no env fallback and no stdin alternative.

Make it optional and read the key from stdin when the flag is absent, following the
convention that is already there rather than inventing a new one: main() consumes
stdin line 1 as the password before dispatching any subcommand, so the key is line 2.
That mirrors restore-keys, which reads a recovery-phrase line and then a password line.

--private-key is kept working, so existing scripts and an older GUI still run against a
patched engine, but it is documented as the unsafe form. The value is held in a
zeroize::Zeroizing<String> and the parse error deliberately does not echo it, because
callers log stderr.
